WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.440
How are you making sure that your highly sensitive&nbsp;
information is protected while you're running it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.440
Hi my name is Alex Greer from the IBM Cloud&nbsp;
team, and make sure to like and subscribe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.680
So, before I get started talking about&nbsp;
confidential computing I want to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.400
a little bit about why it's such an exciting&nbsp;
field. So, confidential computing among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.240
reasons is exciting for the fact that we're seeing&nbsp;
cross-collaboration in the tech space to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.000
drive the technology forward, and so it's awesome&nbsp;
to see people reaching across a competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.720
aisle to do that, a lot of bright minds. The&nbsp;
second is that the technology is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.000
directly complement the existing data encryption&nbsp;
paradigm that we have today and make it an even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.680
more complete end-to-end story. So, before I get&nbsp;
into the actual technology and some of the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.440
and value behind it let's start with the&nbsp;
existing pillars of data encryption today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.320
So you start with protecting your data while&nbsp;
it's at-rest, so when you're storing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:01.600
So we've got at-rest, you can think of this as&nbsp;
whatever information you'd like to. We'll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.160
represent it simply here. Now we have information&nbsp;
that we want to transit from point-to-point. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.440
in order to do that securely we need to protect&nbsp;
it while it's in-transit, so we say in-transit.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.440
But what's missing in today's&nbsp;
story is this third pillar here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.840
What are you doing to protect it&nbsp;
while you're actually running it?

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.240
The groups you're going to have to protect&nbsp;
yourself against, one, are malicious actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.920
who want to do things like scrape that data.&nbsp;
You're also going to want to protect against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.360
memory dumps, things of that nature. We have the&nbsp;
inevitable threat that we have to protect against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.920
which is insider threats. And, in addition,&nbsp;
we also have a lot of collaboration that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.440
want to go on between us and either a trusted&nbsp;
vendor or even a trusted technology partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.280
but at the same time we really don't want to&nbsp;
expose a piece of highly sensitive information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.320
to them even though we want them to&nbsp;
be able to take advantage of it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:11.920
we've got also our partners and vendors here. How&nbsp;
can we ensure that this information is not only,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.760
not visible to these parties, but also protected&nbsp;
from the worst case scenario? That's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.960
confidential computing comes in. So, let's talk&nbsp;
about what confidential computing looks like.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.600
We earlier talked about the collaboration&nbsp;
across technology leaders in the space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.480
we at IBM are a part of the Confidential Computing&nbsp;
Consortium. So the definition that we follow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.840
confidential computing is as such, confidential&nbsp;
computing is a hardware-based technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.880
allows for the physical partitioning of memory&nbsp;
at the server level. So let's draw our stack.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.160
We've got our hardware level on the&nbsp;
bottom which is where the actual physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.840
partitioning of the memory is going to take place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.840
and then we have the middleware level, and then&nbsp;
for the example but not exclusive to we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:07.040
to talk about any containerized abstraction of&nbsp;
this. So we're going to have containers here.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.000
So what's taking place is that at the hardware&nbsp;
level we have that physical partitioning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.880
memory which allows for you to actually&nbsp;
run that application in its own silo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.720
So the silo in the scenario that we've&nbsp;
painted here is going to be called an enclave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.560
So we've got an enclave here, one, two,&nbsp;
three, etcetera, etcetera, etcetera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:37.280
etcetera. So these enclaves can have applications&nbsp;
run in that that physically isolated environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.800
but let's take a look into more detail&nbsp;
about what that actual enclave is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.560
So the enclave itself functions like a black&nbsp;
box so to speak. This black box or enclave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.000
has that data that we spoke about earlier we'll&nbsp;
make it the same one from our previous example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.400
but what it also has in here are the set of&nbsp;
techniques or the things that or the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.560
processing and the procedures for that processing&nbsp;
that information so we've got our techniques here.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.400
What this system does in a scenario in&nbsp;
which we had the malicious actor from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:22.920
earlier, we had the insider threat,&nbsp;
and we even had our own partners --

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.640
what it does is it has an encryption key that&nbsp;
it only extends out to the authorized program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.760
That allows for that authorized program to decrypt&nbsp;
the information running within this physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:44.320
isolated silo and to be able to actually perform&nbsp;
its set of processes. So, this authorized program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:50.800
can do so, but that key is not extended to&nbsp;
the other parties. So that right privilege,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.080
since it's not offered here, prevents the access&nbsp;
from for a malicious actor. It prevents the access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.960
to an insider threat. And then finally,&nbsp;
it even prevents access to a partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.840
What actual access is it preventing now? This&nbsp;
is access to modify that code as well as and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:14.000
to view that data while it's actually inside this&nbsp;
physically partitioned silo. But what's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.680
important for our design is that we verify&nbsp;
that the interaction with that code or data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.800
was what we hope for it to be. So&nbsp;
we need to have what we refer to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:31.760
attestation reports. So attestation reports&nbsp;
you can see here, attestation reports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.880
And just for good measure, we've got in here&nbsp;
that encryption key that we talked about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:46.080
So let's get back to the key value proposition we&nbsp;
just discussed. So what this secure enclave this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.840
black box allowed us to do, it gave us a data&nbsp;
and code integrity that we didn't have before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.000
One, it reduced the visibility of&nbsp;
that data while it was being run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.280
only to the the authorized program&nbsp;
itself. So, we have restricted visibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.920
The second, is that it took that data and&nbsp;
it actually prevented these parties from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.080
making any sort of undue modification&nbsp;
to the actual code itself. So no mod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.680
or unauthorized mod, and then what we were able&nbsp;
to do was verify the actual interaction with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.960
code and that data via attestation reports which&nbsp;
is very critical for corroborating the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.720
that our system is telling us. So now let's&nbsp;
look at the use cases that have been enabled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.640
So the first use case here we're going&nbsp;
to talk about is multiparty computing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.200
Multiparty computing coming down to right here&nbsp;
we're actually working with the technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.080
partner, it allows for us to take highly sensitive&nbsp;
information and exchange it with other parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.440
without actually exposing it so therefore what we&nbsp;
can do is we can share data sets, we can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:04.080
collaborate, and perform functions commonly on top&nbsp;
of highly sensitive information. So you can think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.560
about maybe a collaboration between two research&nbsp;
institutions who otherwise in the past had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.600
go around a very complicated path for actually&nbsp;
getting approval to exchange that information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.080
now they've got that quick and easy path&nbsp;
by simply not allowing that information to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.680
be exposed to the other party. The next case&nbsp;
that we're going to talk about is IP. So IP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.000
you can think of that next great discovery&nbsp;
that your company is making perhaps you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.840
discovered some sort of pharmaceutical&nbsp;
innovation and you have the actual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.200
the blend or the chemical composition and the&nbsp;
other things that are unique about your solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:41.520
and you're wanting to actually share that with&nbsp;
another party, but again without exposing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.040
information to them. So this is another&nbsp;
great scenario where you're protected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:52.240
Now the final one, it's a real situation that we&nbsp;
all have to tackle today is that we give the keys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.360
to the kingdom essentially to the people that we&nbsp;
trust and we hire, but an insider thread is always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.040
a possibility. So we have to protect our workloads&nbsp;
from that possibility. So we have insider threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:09.120
here and now we've protected ourselves from even&nbsp;
the case in which our own turn on us. So now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.600
that we've got the use cases laid out you can see&nbsp;
the clear value that is provided by confidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.920
computing. Confidential computing is focused on&nbsp;
protecting application data while you're actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:26.000
running it, and it allows for us to be able to&nbsp;
collaborate more freely with other parties as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.280
as protect ourselves in a new way from malicious&nbsp;
actors whether external or internal. Thank you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.920
listening. If you have questions please drop us&nbsp;
a line below. If you want to see more videos like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.920
this in the future, please like and subscribe.&nbsp;
